Hi [~accountid:712020:f1fcbe05-e4af-4cd0-b974-656802946f17] ,

There’s no hard legal blocker under Croatian or EU law to introducing a “Croatian-speaking” ride category, but it must be carefully framed and implemented to avoid discrimination or indirect restriction of market access for foreign drivers.

A language requirement is not in itself discriminatory if it serves a legitimate purpose (e.g. ensuring clear communication with local riders) and is applied proportionately.

These are the things that you need keep in mind: 

# Do not name the category  “Croatian” or “Local” - but “Croatian-speaking” (or something similar;
# Apply a standardised language test/interview with clear, documented criteria;
# Ensure equal access for any driver, including non-EU nationals with valid work/ride access;
# Avoid automated exclusion of foreign-registered drivers; access should depend solely on verified proficiency;
# Maintain same pricing and commission as Standard to avoids potential unfair commercial practice or indirect price discrimination

