🚨 {color:#ff5630}+*Huawei’s version includes a change to Clause 6.2.*+ {color}

* The clause extends Bolt’s *liability period from 1 year to 2 years* (i.e. the Customer can bring claims up to 24 months after the issue arises), whereas the standard Bolt template allows for claims to be brought only within 12 months.
* {color:#0747a6}Importantly, the {color}{color:#0747a6}*financial cap on liability remains unchanged*{color}{color:#0747a6} – it is still limited to the amount paid by the Customer in the {color}{color:#0747a6}*3-month period prior to the claim*{color}{color:#0747a6}.{color}

*Risk / Impact:*

* This change *does not increase the value of Bolt’s liability* — only the *time period* during which a claim can be brought.
* It does introduce a slightly longer “tail risk”: a Customer could initiate a claim well after the service relationship has ended (longer claim window may add operational risk to be considered).
* From a legal perspective, this is a *minor risk*, and there’s no increased commercial liability beyond the 3-month fee cap. But flagging for your and [~accountid:5b0fdc5182e05b22cc7d4cf3] attention. 

{color:#ff5630}+*Clause 7.2 – Termination Notice Period Extended*+{color}
The standard Bolt template allows either party to terminate the agreement at any time with *7 days' notice*. In the Huawei version, this has been amended to *30 days' notice*.

* {color:#0747a6}This change is fine subject to {color}[~accountid:5b0fdc5182e05b22cc7d4cf3]{color:#0747a6} 's approval. {color}

