Hi [~accountid:640b2a022847866310028a34] 
*This should not be signed and I cannot see its relevance.* It’s not simply a supplier info form or anything administrative — it appears to be a generic third-party supplier agreement that Plum uses in the course of its own business operations. 

If we were to sign the agreement, *Bolt would then be contractually bound to all of the below obligations* — {color:#ff5630}which are of course {color}{color:#ff5630}*completely irrelevant to the Bolt Business relationship being undertaken*{color}{color:#ff5630}, {color}but would nevertheless *expose Bolt to enforceable contractual obligations*. This would *place Bolt at risk of being in breach* if those (inapplicable) supplier duties were not met, +regardless of the mismatch in commercial context+.

*Basically, by signing it will bind Bolt to:*

* Provide ongoing access to Bolt’s rates and product content;
* Undertake and maintain API/XML integrations at Bolt’s expense;
* Allow Plum to add a margin and resell Bolt services under the Plum brand;
* Permit Plum to collect and control customer payments before remitting net amounts to Bolt;
* Operate under an evergreen agreement with only 30 days’ notice to terminate;
* Accept a lack of commercial protections (no warranties, liability caps, service standards, or termination for cause).

{color:#0747a6}*In the circumstances I’ve drafted a brief note that you can relay back to the customer:*{color}
{color:#0747a6}_Thank you for sharing the agreement. After internal review, we’ve confirmed that the document provided is not appropriate for the nature of our relationship under the Bolt Business offering and introduces obligations that are not applicable to our intended engagement. For that reason, we’re unable to sign this agreement. We’re of course happy to continue engaging with Plum through the standard Bolt Business channels and terms or provide any company details you may require._{color}

